### The short version of how fifteen years of "unrelated" diagnoses turned out to be one story, told here so the rest of this series doesn't require homework.

> *Originally published on Substack. Imported to My Ledger as part of the adrenal journey series.*

Some of you found this Substack through v64otd.com, where I write about corporations, government, and the space where those two things get a little too friendly. If that’s you, you may already know this story from a piece called “Fifteen Years, Six Specialists, Zero Questions,” which still lives there in full. Consider this the Substack version — same story, so this series has its own starting point here too.

For fifteen years, I collected diagnoses. High blood pressure. Type 2 diabetes. High cholesterol. Sleep apnea. Vein problems in my twenties that usually show up decades later. Cataracts way earlier than they’re supposed to happen. A body that ran cold in my hands and hot everywhere else at the wrong times. Each one got its own doctor, its own prescription, its own line item on the chart. Nobody ever stepped back and asked whether five or six “unrelated” problems showing up in the same person might share one cause, because nobody’s job was to ask that question. Everybody’s job was to treat the one thing in front of them, competently, in the fifteen minutes they had, and move to the next patient.

I’d been telling doctors as far back as 2008 or 2009 that something was driving all of it, not just producing it piece by piece. For years, none of them listened or looked past whatever symptom was directly in front of them. I got sideways with doctors over it on two separate occasions before I finally insisted on expanded testing myself. That’s an uncomfortable thing to admit is necessary — but it’s apparently how you have to navigate your own health care in this country. You push, or you wait.

It took a cardiologist looking at bloodwork he wasn’t even the specialist for, telling me the pattern looked like something nobody had connected, and telling me to go figure it out myself, which resources to use, etc, because he didn’t have time to do it in a cardiology appointment and is outside his specialty. So I did — with a lot of help from AI to organize fifteen years of scattered results into something that finally made sense. That work led to a test nobody had ever ordered, which led to another test, which led to imaging, which found something real: masses on both of my adrenal glands. Not a hunch. Not small tumors, either — the size suggested they’d been there for years, maybe decades. A thing on a scan that a doctor could point to.

That’s where this chapter ends. I’ve since had a consultation with an endocrine surgery team to figure out what happens next, and that’s what the rest of this series will cover — not on the main site, which stays where I do the corporate accountability and other stuff, but here, where I can be more personal about something that’s, well, personal.

A few things before I keep going. I’m not writing this for sympathy, and I’m not looking for medical advice in the comments — I’ve got people helping me handle what comes next. I’m writing it because the structural problem underneath my own story — a system built around fifteen-minute visits and single-organ specialties, where nobody’s incentivized to look at the whole picture — isn’t unique to me, and I’d bet money some of you reading this have your own version of it sitting in your own chart, unquestioned. If any part of this sounded familiar, that’s not a coincidence. That’s the point.

Chapter 2 is up next, covering the surgical consult, a committee I never sat in on, and what happened the day after. I’ll keep the clinical detail light and the honesty heavy, same as always.

